site people, both physically and culturally, with members originally hailing from all the surrounding areas. What language do Konso speak? The Konso speak the Konso language (Afaa Xonso) as a mother tongue. It belongs to the Cushitic branch of the Afro-Asiatic family. Konso is divided into four dialects: Kholme, Duuro, Fasha and Karatti. It shares a close lexical similarity with Dirasha, and is today transcribed using the Ethiopic script. Where do Konso live? Konso typically live in large towns, each governed by a council of elders. A few can also be found in parts of northern Kenya. The Konso speak the Konso language (Afaa Xonso) as a mother tongue. It belongs to the Cushitic branch of the Afro-Asiatic family. What do Konso people look like? According to Hallpike (1972), Konso family traditions indicate that they are a composite people, both physically and culturally, with members originally hailing from all the surrounding areas. In terms of physical appearance, the Konso tend to be small and wiry, with high cheekbones and pointed chins.",Konso,Afro-Asiatic
